\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Cylinder positivity of the circle field}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Fix #{m>0}, and let the coordinate process of \ref{fdip-00BC}
be extended to the circle by \ref{fdip-00BE}. Let
#{t_1,\ldots,t_n} satisfy #{0\le t_j\le 1/2}, and let #{F} be
a bounded measurable function of #{n} real variables. Then
##{\mathbb E\Bigl(\overline{F\bigl(X(-t_1),\ldots,X(-t_n)\bigr)}\,F\bigl(X(t_1),\ldots,X(t_n)\bigr)\Bigr)\ge 0.}
Fixed coordinates at #{0} and at #{1/2} may be included among
the arguments of #{F} on both sides.}

\proof{
\p{If every time equals #{0}, or every time equals #{1/2}, the
two sides use the same sample and the integrand is the square
of the absolute value of #{F}. The expectation is nonnegative.
In the remaining case at least two distinct points occur.}

\p{In that remaining case, suppose that every #{t_j} is dyadic and that the
points #{t_j} and #{-t_j} are distinct except at the fixed
points. The finite set they form is invariant under
#{x\mapsto -x}, and \ref{fdip-00FP} is the stated inequality.
If some non-fixed times coincide, pass to the distinct subset
and read #{F} as constant in the repeated coordinates.}

\p{For arbitrary real times, choose dyadic #{t_j^{(N)}} in the
same range, with #{t_j^{(N)}\to t_j}, leaving a time equal to
#{0} or to #{1/2} unchanged. The version of \ref{fdip-00BE}
has continuous paths, so the samples converge almost surely.
A bounded continuous function of those samples is dominated by
its supremum norm, and each dyadic expectation is nonnegative.
Dominated convergence gives the inequality for every bounded
continuous #{F}.}

\p{Let #{\mu} be the centered law of
#{(X(t_1),\ldots,X(t_n))}. It is the image of a standard
Gaussian on #{\mathbb R^k} under an injective linear map,
where #{k} is the rank of the covariance. Hermite polynomials
are an orthogonal basis of the square-integrable functions of
that standard Gaussian. Injectivity realizes each of them as a
polynomial in these #{n} coordinates, so polynomials are dense
in #{L^2(\mu)}. For a polynomial #{p} and a radius #{R}, let
#{\chi_R} be continuous, equal to #{1} on the ball of radius
#{R}, and equal to #{0} outside the ball of radius #{R+1}.
The product #{\chi_R p} is a bounded continuous function of
the same coordinates, and #{|p|^2} is #{\mu}-integrable, so
the #{L^2(\mu)} distance from #{\chi_R p} to #{p} tends to
#{0} as #{R\to\infty}. Bounded continuous functions of these
coordinates are therefore dense in #{L^2(\mu)}. The form
#{B(F)=\mathbb E\bigl(\overline{F(X(-t))}F(X(t))\bigr)}
satisfies
##{|B(F)-B(G)|\le\|F\|_2\|F-G\|_2+\|G\|_2\|F-G\|_2,}
because #{t\mapsto -t} preserves the joint samples, by
#{r(s)=r(1-s)} in \ref{fdip-00FO}. Nonnegativity on the
bounded continuous functions extends to every class in
#{L^2(\mu)}, including every bounded measurable #{F}.}
}
